        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>Optic disc edema develops in approximately 16% of astronauts during long-duration spaceflight and is of high concern to the NASA medical community because of potentially irreversible vision loss during exploration-class spaceflight missions. Currently there is not a reliable method to predict which crewmembers will develop disc edema; however, recently, a novel ground-based spaceflight analog, consisting of strict 6° head-down tilt (HDT) bed rest, was developed that reproduced disc edema in healthy test subjects that can be used to test novel pathophysiological hypotheses and possible Spaceflight Associated Neuro-ocular Syndrome (SANS) countermeasures.<br><br>The goal of this experiment is to identify underlying mechanisms of optic disc edema in a ground-based analog and test an operationally relevant countermeasure (CM) to prevent it from developing. <br>To meet this goal, the experiment will address two specific aims.<br>Aim 1: Determine if donning veno-occlusive thigh cuffs (VTC) during and for 120 minutes after daily aerobic exercise can be used to (a) reverse headward fluid shift from bed rest for a sustained period of time and (b) to prevent ocular structural and functional changes from developing at the end of bed rest<br>Aim 2: Determine the role altered vasculature in the development of optic disc edema in the spaceflight analog.<br><br>APPROACH:<br>This study is part of a larger study that will test multiple countermeasures. The larger study will include 48 human subjects separated into four groups (12 subjects per group): one control groups defined by NASA and three countermeasure treatment groups. The control group will remain in strict 6° HDT bedrest with no countermeasures. The three countermeasure treatment groups include (1) daily exposure to exercise with VTCs during HDT, (2) strict HDT bed rest with six hours of 25 mmHg LBNP, daily, during strict HDT bedrest, and (3) six hours of upright (seated) posture, daily, during the strict HDT bedrest portion of the study. The experiment described here will focus on the effects of exercise plus VTCs.<br><br>To address Aim 1, the researchers will determine femoral artery conductance before and up to 120 minutes after cessation of aerobic exercise before, during, and after bed rest. Femoral vein cross sectional area, internal jugular pressure, and vein cross sectional area will be used to quantify the amount of blood trapped in the periphery versus above the level of the heart. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) imaging will be used to identify structural and functional changes to the optic nerve and retinal nerve fiber layer (RNFL) of the retina. Total retinal thickness (TRT) will quantify the effectiveness of the CMs applied in this study. Minimum rim thickness (MRW) and RNFL thickness measurements will allow for direct comparison of change in TRT between subjects participating in this study, previous bed rest campaigns, and astronauts who have flown on the ISS. Full-field flash electroretinography (ERG) and pattern ERG (PERG) will be used to directly measure the electrical activity within the eye in order to detect subtle abnormalities in visual function that may not otherwise be captured by current clinical metrics.<br><br>To address Aim 2, OCT angiography (OCTA) will be applied over the optic nerve head in subjects before, during, and after bed rest to identify subtle changes in ocular vasculature which may precede or result from the development of optic disc edema. OCTA images of the retinal and choroidal vasculature will be used to create detailed visualizations of the foveal avascular zone (FAZ) and perfusion and vessel density maps, and to monitor the thickness of the choriocapillaris. Intravenous fluorescein angiography (IVFA) will be used to assess retinal vasculature and blood-retinal barrier status immediately after the conclusion of bed rest to determine if the optic disc edema that presents in spaceflight analog subjects disrupts the retinal-blood barrier. Both IVFA and OCTA will be used to evaluate perfusion density and microvasculature integrity around the optic nerve.<br><br>RESULTS:<br>This experiment will apply a novel countermeasure approach to prevent the development of optic disc edema in a ground-based spaceflight analog and identify alterations in microvascular blood flow around the optic nerve, macula, and choroid using OCTA and IVFA. The researchers for this experiment anticipate wearing VTC during and after daily aerobic exercise as a countermeasure will reverse the headward fluid shift that arises from HDT bed rest. As a result, fewer subjects receiving the countermeasure treatment will develop disc edema, and the increase in total retinal thickness will be less compared to the control group. There will also be mitigated loss of electrical optic nerve function and vascular leakage on IVFA in the treatment compared to the control groups.<br>
        </div>

